of Rome", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "diocletianic-persecution", "targetName": "Diocletianic Persecution", "context": "Anthimus of Rome was a victim and a participant in the broader phenomenon of the Diocletianic Persecution, suffering martyrdom during its height."}, {"sourceSlug": "early-christian-church", "sourceName": "Early Christian Church", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "anthimus-of-rome", "targetName": "Anthimus of Rome", "context": "The Early Christian Church, through popular veneration and later formal recognition, canonized Anthimus of Rome as a saint and martyr."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Empire", "role": "Site of martyrdom"}, {"name": "Bithynia, Asia Minor", "role": "Traditional birthplace"}, {"name": "Via Salaria, Roman Empire", "role": "Place of execution"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Anthimus of Rome", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "anthimus-of-rome"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2280870", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthimus_of_Rome"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Anthimus of Rome, though largely legendary, represents the thousands of martyrs whose stories provided a crucial foundation for the Early Christian Church's identity and resilience during the Diocletianic Persecution, shaping its narrative of sacrifice and triumph over imperial power. His veneration fostered regional Christian communities and pilgrimage, leaving a lasting cultural and spiritual footprint.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-16T20:18:41Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35145477430", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Saint Anthimus of Rome, or Sant'Antimo in Italian, is a Christian saint. His life is largely composed of legend. He is said to have been born in Bithynia. A Christian priest, he was imprisoned for his beliefs at the time of the Emperors Diocletian and Maximian. His feast day is May 11.", "newValue": "Saint Anthimus of Rome, or Sant'Antimo in Italian, represents one of the countless Christian martyrs whose lives, though often embellished by later hagiography, underpinned the nascent Church's identity during the brutal Diocletianic Persecution (303–313 CE). A Christian priest said to have originated from Bithynia in Asia Minor, Anthimus became a pivotal figure in the collective memory of early Roman Christianity, embodying steadfast faith in the face of imperial tyranny. His veneration, particularly in Italy, cemented his place as a patron saint and a symbol of spiritual resistance against the late Roman state's final, desperate attempt to eradicate the burgeoning religion.

According to tradition, Anthimus was apprehended and imprisoned for his unwavering Christian beliefs under the co-emperors Diocletian and Maximian, whose policies mandated the systematic suppression of Christian practice and the destruction of sacred texts. His legend describes miraculous conversions within the prison, including that of the prison warden Pinianus, and even members of the imperial household like the priest Maximinus and the virgin Lucina, who provided burial for the martyrs. Eventually, Anthimus was martyred by decapitation on the Via Salaria outside Rome in 303 CE, his execution serving as a stark warning to other Christians, although it ultimately failed to deter the faith's spread.

The enduring legacy of Anthimus of Rome lies not in verifiable historical records of his personal actions, but in the powerful narrative of martyrdom that his story, alongside those of countless others, contributed to the Christian tradition. These legends inspired generations of believers, reinforced communal identity, and provided a heroic template for Christian devotion and sacrifice. His feast day on May 11 continues to be observed, particularly within the Catholic Church, connecting contemporary adherents to the foundational struggles of the early faith."}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-16T20:18:41Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35145477430", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The systematic persecution of Christians under the Roman Emperors Diocletian and Maximian directly led to Anthimus's arrest and martyrdom.", "The rapid growth and increasing visibility of Christianity within the Roman Empire created a perceived threat to traditional Roman polytheism and imperial authority.", "The theological development of Christian hagiography and the veneration of martyrs provided a framework for documenting and celebrating figures like Anthimus."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-09-16T20:18:41Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#35145477430", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "303 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks that shaped subsequent civili", "type": "Movement", "year": "303 CE"}], "newValue": ["The legend of Anthimus of Rome contributed to the rich body of Christian hagiography that inspired devotion and solidified the identity of the early Church.", "